WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.357 --> 00:00:01.440
MARTIN OMANDER: Hi, Aaron.

00:00:01.440 --> 00:00:03.160
How are you doing?

00:00:03.160 --> 00:00:05.280
AARON WANJALA: I'm
doing great, thanks.

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:06.947
MARTIN OMANDER: I've
been asked to start

00:00:06.947 --> 00:00:09.960
building a new inventory
tracking system on Cloud Run.

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:12.030
We use Cloud Run,
which is serverless,

00:00:12.030 --> 00:00:14.520
to reduce the Ops work needed.

00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:17.430
The application will use
Java and Spring Boot,

00:00:17.430 --> 00:00:20.543
but I'm not sure how to get
that started on Cloud Run.

00:00:20.543 --> 00:00:21.960
AARON WANJALA: No
worries, Martin.

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:23.650
It's actually pretty
simple these days.

00:00:23.650 --> 00:00:24.317
Let me show you.

00:00:24.317 --> 00:00:27.726
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:34.210 --> 00:00:37.000
So remind me again, what kind
of app were we building here?

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:38.750
MARTIN OMANDER: It's
a pretty standard web

00:00:38.750 --> 00:00:40.620
app with an HTML
user interface and it

00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:42.468
will connect to a database.

00:00:42.468 --> 00:00:43.510
AARON WANJALA: All right.

00:00:43.510 --> 00:00:46.870
Well, first things first, how
about we get a simple service

00:00:46.870 --> 00:00:48.520
created and deployed?

00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:50.488
MARTIN OMANDER: Sounds
like a good start to me.

00:00:50.488 --> 00:00:52.780
AARON WANJALA: We'll start
by making a new Google Cloud

00:00:52.780 --> 00:00:55.327
Platform project for your app.

00:00:55.327 --> 00:00:56.410
MARTIN OMANDER: All right.

00:00:56.410 --> 00:01:00.000
And we can call it
inventory-tracking-app.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:01.850
AARON WANJALA: Perfect.

00:01:01.850 --> 00:01:04.762
Once that's created, we can
open up the Cloud Shell.

00:01:04.762 --> 00:01:07.220
MARTIN OMANDER: Do we need to
use the Cloud Shell for this,

00:01:07.220 --> 00:01:08.087
though?

00:01:08.087 --> 00:01:09.920
AARON WANJALA: No,
that's entirely optional.

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:12.788
As long as you have the gcloud
command-line tool installed,

00:01:12.788 --> 00:01:15.080
you'll be able to do everything
from your local machine

00:01:15.080 --> 00:01:17.137
if you prefer to do that.

00:01:17.137 --> 00:01:18.220
MARTIN OMANDER: Excellent.

00:01:18.220 --> 00:01:22.010
Well, I will use the Cloud Shell
to get started for convenience.

00:01:22.010 --> 00:01:26.350
I'm used to using the wizard
at start.spring.io to get

00:01:26.350 --> 00:01:28.527
new Spring Boot applications.

00:01:28.527 --> 00:01:30.110
AARON WANJALA: That
should work great.

00:01:30.110 --> 00:01:32.120
All we'll need is
the web dependency.

00:01:32.120 --> 00:01:34.700
It may be easier to use the
command-line version of that

00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:37.040
to get the ZIP file in your
Cloud Shell environment.

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:38.090
I sent you the command.

00:01:38.090 --> 00:01:39.410
Did you get it?

00:01:39.410 --> 00:01:41.300
MARTIN OMANDER: Yep, I see it.

00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:45.360
All right, let me run it.

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:48.390
Looks like I got a ZIP
file with a starter project

00:01:48.390 --> 00:01:51.450
like I'm used to
from start.spring.io.

00:01:51.450 --> 00:01:55.980
I'll just unzip it here
and open the editor.

00:01:55.980 --> 00:01:57.840
OK, all set.

00:01:57.840 --> 00:01:58.925
What's next, Aaron?

00:01:58.925 --> 00:02:00.300
AARON WANJALA:
Well, that command

00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:01.650
gave us a good starting point.

00:02:01.650 --> 00:02:04.140
But we'll need something
to give us a sign of life

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:06.130
once we're running this.

00:02:06.130 --> 00:02:09.595
Let's add a Hello World
endpoint to help us with that.

00:02:09.595 --> 00:02:10.470
MARTIN OMANDER: Sure.

00:02:10.470 --> 00:02:13.440
I'll just add a
HelloController here.

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:15.900
It will return a
placeholder inventory

00:02:15.900 --> 00:02:19.260
count of a placeholder
product for now,

00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:22.020
and this is just so we can see
that all parts of the system

00:02:22.020 --> 00:02:23.100
are working.

00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:26.010
Now to get started, how
can we get this running

00:02:26.010 --> 00:02:27.600
from the command line?

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:28.920
AARON WANJALA: We can
actually run it the same way

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:30.000
you would on your machine.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:31.410
Then we can hit
the Preview button

00:02:31.410 --> 00:02:32.730
in the top right
of the Cloud Shell

00:02:32.730 --> 00:02:33.813
to see it in your browser.

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:37.500
MARTIN OMANDER: Mhm.

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:38.250
There it is.

00:02:38.250 --> 00:02:39.210
Very nice.

00:02:39.210 --> 00:02:41.880
So the application is
running from my command line.

00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:44.880
I can use this for
testing as I build it out.

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:48.010
Now how can we get this deployed
to Cloud Run up in the cloud

00:02:48.010 --> 00:02:49.960
so others can test
the application?

00:02:49.960 --> 00:02:51.460
AARON WANJALA: In
the simplest case,

00:02:51.460 --> 00:02:55.197
we can do it in one line
with gcloud run deploy.

00:02:55.197 --> 00:02:56.030
MARTIN OMANDER: Wow.

00:02:56.030 --> 00:02:58.400
That is a short command.

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:00.710
But is there a way I can
see what kind of magic

00:03:00.710 --> 00:03:03.077
is happening behind
the scenes here?

00:03:03.077 --> 00:03:04.160
AARON WANJALA: Absolutely.

00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:05.618
You'll be prompted
for a few things

00:03:05.618 --> 00:03:08.930
during the run deploy command,
like providing the source code

00:03:08.930 --> 00:03:11.720
location, providing
the service name,

00:03:11.720 --> 00:03:14.840
enabling the Cloud Run
API, selecting your region,

00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:16.430
and enabling Artifact Registry.

00:03:16.430 --> 00:03:17.870
MARTIN OMANDER: Hmm, I see.

00:03:17.870 --> 00:03:19.280
I did that.

00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:23.000
Now I'm being asked if I want to
enable unauthenticated requests

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:24.327
to this service?

00:03:24.327 --> 00:03:25.160
AARON WANJALA: Yeah.

00:03:25.160 --> 00:03:26.618
To make it easy to
test here, we'll

00:03:26.618 --> 00:03:29.090
want to allow
unauthenticated requests.

00:03:29.090 --> 00:03:30.770
After all that
enabling is done, it'll

00:03:30.770 --> 00:03:32.730
start the actual deploy process.

00:03:32.730 --> 00:03:35.250
Now, a lot is going on behind
the scenes of this command,

00:03:35.250 --> 00:03:37.880
which you don't need to know,
but you might find interesting.

00:03:37.880 --> 00:03:39.255
What's happening
during this step

00:03:39.255 --> 00:03:42.200
is that Cloud Build zips and
saves your source in a Cloud

00:03:42.200 --> 00:03:45.560
Storage bucket, uses the Cloud
Native Computing Foundation

00:03:45.560 --> 00:03:48.080
Buildpacks in the background
to build your image,

00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:50.630
creates a registry to store
the resulting container

00:03:50.630 --> 00:03:53.480
image, if not already present,
and creates a Cloud Run

00:03:53.480 --> 00:03:56.377
service to host your app, if
that's not already present.

00:03:56.377 --> 00:03:57.210
MARTIN OMANDER: Wow.

00:03:57.210 --> 00:04:01.530
And, just like that, with one
single command, we're live, eh?

00:04:01.530 --> 00:04:02.755
AARON WANJALA: Yes, we are.

00:04:02.755 --> 00:04:04.630
MARTIN OMANDER: Well,
that was pretty simple.

00:04:04.630 --> 00:04:07.200
But for my app to
get to production,

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:08.760
I'm going to need some tests.

00:04:08.760 --> 00:04:12.135
And I'll want them to
pass before I deploy.

00:04:12.135 --> 00:04:13.260
AARON WANJALA: Fair enough.

00:04:13.260 --> 00:04:15.311
We can get that going
pretty quick, as well.

00:04:15.311 --> 00:04:16.769
MARTIN OMANDER:
OK, so why don't we

00:04:16.769 --> 00:04:19.980
get started by adding
a simple failing test?

00:04:19.980 --> 00:04:23.040
The test checks if
1 plus 1 equals 3.

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:24.330
And it will fail, of course.

00:04:24.330 --> 00:04:25.810
And then I will fix it.

00:04:25.810 --> 00:04:29.910
And if all that works, I
can add real tests later on.

00:04:29.910 --> 00:04:30.840
AARON WANJALA: Great.

00:04:30.840 --> 00:04:32.100
Once that test is
done, we'll have

00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:33.517
to inform Cloud
Build that we want

00:04:33.517 --> 00:04:35.910
to treat running those
steps as their own step

00:04:35.910 --> 00:04:38.307
and to stop if they fail.

00:04:38.307 --> 00:04:39.890
MARTIN OMANDER: How
much config are we

00:04:39.890 --> 00:04:41.240
looking at for this, Aaron?

00:04:41.240 --> 00:04:44.090
AARON WANJALA: One file and
just a handful of lines.

00:04:44.090 --> 00:04:46.010
I'll send you the
Cloud Build YAML.

00:04:46.010 --> 00:04:49.280
This file tells Cloud
Build to run your tests,

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:51.740
install the project's
Maven dependencies,

00:04:51.740 --> 00:04:54.290
build the image using
gcloud build submit

00:04:54.290 --> 00:04:57.077
and the --pack flag
to select Buildpacks,

00:04:57.077 --> 00:04:58.910
and then deploy the
resulting image to Cloud

00:04:58.910 --> 00:05:01.430
Build with gcloud run deploy.

00:05:01.430 --> 00:05:02.990
MARTIN OMANDER: OK,
that makes sense.

00:05:02.990 --> 00:05:07.410
Now, how do we use it to run
the tests before deployment?

00:05:07.410 --> 00:05:10.080
AARON WANJALA: Well, since we
have a Cloud Build YAML file

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:11.820
present in our
source directory, we

00:05:11.820 --> 00:05:15.180
can just run gcloud build submit
and it will look for that file

00:05:15.180 --> 00:05:16.410
by default.

00:05:16.410 --> 00:05:18.405
MARTIN OMANDER: Great.

00:05:18.405 --> 00:05:20.970
Mhm.

00:05:20.970 --> 00:05:22.750
And, as expected, it failed.

00:05:22.750 --> 00:05:23.250
Good.

00:05:25.407 --> 00:05:26.240
AARON WANJALA: Yeah.

00:05:26.240 --> 00:05:27.770
Now we can fix it
and rest assured

00:05:27.770 --> 00:05:31.220
that we won't be pushing broken
builds to prod down the line.

00:05:31.220 --> 00:05:32.570
MARTIN OMANDER: Very good.

00:05:32.570 --> 00:05:35.300
Next, I'd add my code
to source control.

00:05:35.300 --> 00:05:37.430
Then I'd like to
automatically run the tests

00:05:37.430 --> 00:05:40.250
and deploy to my Cloud
Run test environment

00:05:40.250 --> 00:05:43.460
whenever code is committed
to the dev branch.

00:05:43.460 --> 00:05:45.540
AARON WANJALA: Those sound
like great next steps.

00:05:45.540 --> 00:05:47.850
Let me show you how to do
them in the next video.

00:05:47.850 --> 00:05:49.850
MARTIN OMANDER: Thank you
for showing me, Aaron.

00:05:49.850 --> 00:05:51.990
And thank you,
everyone, for watching.

